CAT = "dispTech"; SEG = "TEC"
Q = [
("For 911 systems, which data element identifies the calling party and may serve as the callback number?",
 "ANI", ["ALI", "ECRF", "PIDF-LO"],
 "47 CFR 9.3 defines Automatic Number Identification as identifying the calling party, usable as the callback number. ALI carries the caller's location."),
("Which E911 data element lets responders identify the geographic location of the calling party?",
 "ALI", ["ANI", "Pseudo-ANI", "AEAN"],
 "Automatic Location Information is transmitted with E911 calls so responders can identify the caller's geographic location. ANI identifies the calling party."),
("A number with as many digits as ANI, but not a directory number, used to convey special meaning, is:",
 "Pseudo-ANI", ["Callback number", "AEAN", "Ported number"],
 "47 CFR 9.3 defines pseudo-ANI as a number of the same length as ANI that is not a North American Numbering Plan directory number and conveys a special meaning by agreement."),
("Wireless Phase I E911 delivers the callback number and the location of the:",
 "Receiving cell site or base station", ["Handset by its latitude and longitude", "Subscriber's billing address", "Caller's registered location"],
 "Under 47 CFR 9.10(d), Phase I gives the PSAP the caller's number and the location of the cell site or base station receiving the call. Coordinates come with Phase II."),
("Wireless Phase II E911 delivers the caller's location as:",
 "Longitude and latitude", ["Cell site and sector", "Billing address", "Registered location"],
 "47 CFR 9.10(e) defines Phase II as the location of 911 calls by longitude and latitude, to the FCC's accuracy rules. Cell site location is Phase I."),
("Handset-based Phase II accuracy is 50 meters for 67% of calls and what distance for 90% of calls?",
 "150 meters", ["100 meters", "150 meters", "300 meters", "500 meters"],
 "47 CFR 9.10(h) sets handset-based accuracy at 50 meters for 67% and 150 meters for 90% of calls. 100 and 300 meters are the network-based standards.", "fixed"),
("Network-based Phase II accuracy is 100 meters for 67% of calls and what distance for 90% of calls?",
 "300 meters", ["150 meters", "300 meters", "500 meters", "1,000 meters"],
 "47 CFR 9.10(h) sets network-based accuracy at 100 meters for 67% and 300 meters for 90% of calls. 150 meters is the handset-based 90% figure.", "fixed"),
("FCC rules count a Phase II location fix toward accuracy only if it arrives within what time of the 911 call?",
 "30 seconds", ["10 seconds", "30 seconds", "60 seconds", "90 seconds"],
 "The FCC's maximum latency, or time to first fix, is 30 seconds from the start of the 911 call until the fix reaches the location information center.", "fixed"),
("The FCC z-axis metric requires vertical location within what distance above or below the handset for 80% of calls?",
 "3 meters", ["1 meter", "3 meters", "5 meters", "10 meters"],
 "47 CFR 9.10(i) sets the z-axis metric at plus or minus 3 meters for 80% of wireless E911 calls from z-axis capable devices.", "fixed"),
("In which form must carriers deliver z-axis information with wireless 911 calls?",
 "Height above ellipsoid", ["Height above ground level", "Floor number only", "Barometric pressure"],
 "Carriers must deliver z-axis information in Height Above Ellipsoid, and add floor level where they have it. Floor level alone does not meet the rule."),
("Under FCC rules, a dispatchable location is a validated street address plus:",
 "Suite or unit details", ["Latitude and longitude", "Cell site and sector", "Billing ZIP code on file"],
 "47 CFR 9.3 defines dispatchable location as the validated street address plus details such as suite or apartment needed to find the caller."),
("Under Kari's Law, a hotel phone that needs \"9\" for an outside line must also reach 911 when the guest dials:",
 "911", ["9-911", "8-911", "0 then 911"],
 "47 CFR 9.16 requires multi-line systems to let users dial 911 directly, with no added digit, code, prefix or post-fix such as the trunk-access digit 9. Extra patterns may also work, but 911 alone must."),
("Kari's Law also requires a multi-line telephone system to send a 911 notification to:",
 "On-site central location", ["Phone carrier's network center", "State 911 board", "Neighboring PSAP"],
 "MLTS notification goes to a central location at the facility or to another person or organization, such as a security desk. It is in addition to the 911 call itself."),
("Under 47 CFR 9.16, MLTS notification must not:",
 "Delay the 911 call", ["Include a callback number", "Go to a security desk", "Use an audible alarm"],
 "MLTS notification must start with the 911 call where feasible, must not delay it, and must go where someone is likely to see or hear it, such as a desk alarm."),
("By what date did on-premises fixed MLTS phones have to provide automated dispatchable location?",
 "January 6, 2021", ["February 16, 2020", "January 6, 2021", "January 6, 2022", "January 18, 2023"],
 "47 CFR 9.16(b)(3)(i) set January 6, 2021 for on-premises fixed phones. Non-fixed and off-premises devices followed on January 6, 2022.", "fixed"),
("The FCC's MLTS 911 rules apply to systems installed or first sold after which date?",
 "February 16, 2020", ["September 30, 2013", "February 16, 2020", "January 6, 2021", "January 6, 2022"],
 "47 CFR 9.17 sets February 16, 2020 as the compliance date for direct dialing and notification on systems made, sold or installed after it.", "fixed"),
("For interconnected VoIP 911, the address a customer gives the provider before service starts is the:",
 "Registered location", ["Dispatchable location", "Service location code", "Billing location"],
 "47 CFR 9.11 requires VoIP providers to get the Registered Location before service starts and give customers a way to update it."),
("A VoIP provider detects a customer calling 911 from somewhere other than the registered address. It must:",
 "Prompt for a new location", ["Block the 911 call", "Route to the statewide PSAP", "Send cell site data"],
 "Under 47 CFR 9.11 the provider must identify use from a different location and either prompt for a new Registered Location or update it automatically."),
("A mobile phone with no valid carrier contract that can still dial 911 is called:",
 "Non-service-initialized", ["Pseudo-ANI-equipped device", "Prepaid handset", "Ported handset"],
 "47 CFR 9.10(o) defines a non-service-initialized handset as one with no valid service contract. NENA calls these NSI devices."),
("Why can't a telecommunicator call back an NSI phone after a disconnect?",
 "It has no callback number", ["Calls roll to voicemail", "Its carrier blocks callbacks", "Its location is withheld"],
 "NSI devices have no subscriber and send no callback number; the FCC's required label warns that the 911 operator cannot call the user back."),
("The answering point a state designates where no local PSAP serves an area is the:",
 "Statewide default answering point", ["Secondary PSAP", "Appropriate local emergency authority", "Regional backup PSAP"],
 "47 CFR 9.3 defines the statewide default answering point as the point designated by the state for the whole state or areas not served by a local PSAP."),
("In NG911, the managed IP network shared by public safety agencies is the:",
 "ESInet", ["ECRF", "ESRP", "LIS"],
 "NENA defines the Emergency Services IP Network as a managed IP network for emergency communications that can be shared by all public safety agencies."),
("Which NG911 element is a SIP proxy that picks the next routing hop based on location and policy?",
 "ESRP", ["ECRF", "ESInet", "PIDF-LO"],
 "The Emergency Service Routing Proxy selects the next hop within the ESInet based on location and policy. The ECRF answers where a location should route."),
("Which NG911 function is a LoST server that maps a caller's location to the right PSAP?",
 "ECRF", ["ESRP", "ACD", "ALI database"],
 "The Emergency Call Routing Function takes a civic address or coordinates and a service URN and returns a URI used to route the call toward the right PSAP."),
("In NG911, the caller's location is carried in a data object called:",
 "PIDF-LO", ["ANI", "Pseudo-ANI", "ECRF"],
 "PIDF-LO is the location object extension of PIDF. NENA says a missing location (ALI or PIDF-LO) should be sought by reverse lookup or from the provider."),
("Sending a wireless 911 call to the PSAP by the handset's own position, not the tower, is called:",
 "Location-based routing", ["Selective routing", "Alternate routing", "Default routing"],
 "47 CFR 9.3 defines location-based routing as using device location information to deliver 911 calls and RTT to the designated answering point."),
("When caller location fails to arrive, NENA says to try a reverse lookup or contact the:",
 "Phone service provider", ["State 911 board", "Neighboring PSAP", "Records management unit"],
 "NENA section 5.7.1 says to determine the location through reverse lookup where permitted, or by contacting the telecommunications service provider."),
("Moving a customer's line between carriers is handled by a neutral third party called the:",
 "Number portability administrator", ["911 system service provider", "Statewide default answering point", "ESInet operator"],
 "NENA section 5.7.2 says porting is done by the Local Number Portability Administrator, so a telecommunicator may need to contact more than one carrier."),
]
